---
/**
 * NewsletterForm: the monthly UK procurement digest signup.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS AS A COMPONENT. The legacy site ships this block twice, on
 * /about ("Get monthly UK procurement digests") and /contact ("Prefer
 * regulatory updates?"), as two independent copies of the same markup. Both
 * were dropped by the Astro rebuild, which is a lead-capture regression rather
 * than a cosmetic one. Restoring it twice by hand is how the legacy tree ended
 * up with two copies that had already drifted apart in id naming, so the form
 * is one component and the surrounding copy stays at the call site, where the
 * two pages genuinely differ.
 *
 * WHERE IT POSTS, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A GUESS.
 *   POST https://app.crowagent.ai/api/notify   {"email": "...", "source": "..."}
 * That is the endpoint both legacy blocks name in their `action`, and it is the
 * one the global interceptor in js/nav-inject.js (~line 1264) actually drives:
 * it intercepts any form whose action matches /api\/notify/, reads the email,
 * and POSTs exactly that JSON body. Verified live on 2026-08-02:
 *   OPTIONS /api/notify -> 204, Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://crowagent.ai,
 *   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: POST, OPTIONS
 * so the cross-origin POST this component makes is the same one production
 * already serves. `form-action 'self' https://app.crowagent.ai` and
 * `connect-src ... https://app.crowagent.ai` in _headers both permit it.
 *
 * SUCCESS IS READ FROM THE RESPONSE, NEVER ASSUMED. An earlier version of the
 * legacy handler sent the request `no-cors` and showed "you are on the list"
 * unconditionally, so while /api/notify 404'd every subscriber saw success and
 * every address was dropped. The rule that came out of that is kept here: a
 * success message is shown only on a 2xx, and any other outcome is visible.
 *
 * PAYLOAD IS DELIBERATELY {email, source} AND NOTHING MORE. The consent line
 * under the form is a notice, not a checkbox, exactly as on the live page, so
 * there is no tick to record. No extra field is invented either: the same
 * reasoning as OA-04 applies, where a `gdpr_consent` field was NOT added to the
 * contact payload unilaterally because a strict server-side schema would then
 * reject every submission on a live capture path.
 *
 * NO PERSONAL DATA GOES ANYWHERE THE NOTICE DOES NOT NAME. This is first-party
 * to CrowAgent, and the address is held in Brevo (EU), which the privacy notice
 * already lists as a sub-processor for "transactional and marketing email
 * delivery". This is not a second OA-08.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE CLOUDFLARE CHECK HERE IS NOT YET VERIFIED BY ANYTHING. Added
 * 2026-08-03 on the owner's instruction that every form carry one. The token is
 * sent as `turnstile_token`, matching the field name /api/contact/submit
 * already verifies, so the platform change is a copy of a block that exists.
 * But `NotifySchema` in web/app/api/notify/route.ts is `{ email, source }` and
 * never reads a token, Zod strips the extra key rather than rejecting it, so
 * sending it is safe on this live capture path but proves nothing today. Until
 * that endpoint calls Cloudflare /siteverify this is client-side friction, not
 * protection: a bot POSTing straight to /api/notify never runs this code. The
 * endpoint's real defence today is the 10-per-hour per-IP rate limit.
 * Logged as OA-32. Do not describe this form as protected until it closes.
 */
import Turnstile from './Turnstile.astro';
import '../../styles/forms.css';
import Button from '../ui/Button.astro';

interface Props {
  /**
   * Unique prefix for this instance's ids. /contact carries the enquiry form as
   * well, so two forms share that document and nothing may collide.
   */
  id: string;
  /**
   * Sent as `source` so signups can be attributed per page. The values match
   * what the legacy interceptor derived from the pathname (`about-newsletter`,
   * `contact-newsletter`), so the existing series does not break in two.
   */
  source: string;
}

const { id, source } = Astro.props;

/** The endpoint both legacy blocks post to. See the note above. */
const NOTIFY_ENDPOINT = 'https://app.crowagent.ai/api/notify';
---

<form
  class="nl"
  method="POST"
  action={NOTIFY_ENDPOINT}
  novalidate
  data-newsletter
  data-source={source}
>
  {/* Honeypot, carried over from the legacy block. Off-screen rather than
      display:none so a bot reading the DOM still finds it. */}
  <div class="form__pot" aria-hidden="true">
    <label for={`${id}-website`}>Website</label>
    <input type="text" name="website" id={`${id}-website`} tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
  </div>

  <div class="nl__row">
    {/* The label is real and associated, just not drawn: a placeholder is not a
        label, and it disappears the moment someone starts typing. */}
    <label class="visually-hidden" for={`${id}-email`}>Email address</label>
    <input
      class="field__input"
      type="email"
      id={`${id}-email`}
      name="email"
      required
      autocomplete="email"
      placeholder="you@company.com"
      aria-describedby={`${id}-err`}
    />
    <Button type="submit" class="nl__submit">Subscribe</Button>
  </div>

  <p class="field__err nl__err" id={`${id}-err`} hidden>
    Please enter a valid email address.
  </p>

  {/* The same Cloudflare check as the other two forms, from the same component.
      See the warning at the top of this file about what verifies it. */}
  <Turnstile id={id} />

  {/* One live region for both outcomes, same as the enquiry form.

      [A-269 2026-09-08] It carries the sending state too now. Both marks are in
      the markup and the state class shows one, so a transition changes a text
      node and a class rather than rebuilding the children of a live region. */}
  <p class="form__status nl__status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden>
    <span class="form__spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <svg class="form__tick" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
    </svg>
    <span class="form__status-text"></span>
  </p>

  <noscript>
    <p class="form__noscript">
      This form needs JavaScript to send. Please email us at
      <a href="mailto:hello@crowagent.ai">hello@crowagent.ai</a>.
    </p>
  </noscript>
</form>

{/* VERBATIM from both legacy blocks, including the link target. It is the only
    thing telling a subscriber what they are agreeing to, so it is not reworded. */}
<p class="nl__legal">
  By subscribing you agree to the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
</p>

<script>
  /** Same definition as the enquiry and partner forms. One rule, site-wide. */
  const EMAIL_RE = /^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$/;

  function initNewsletterForms() {
    const forms = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLFormElement>('form[data-newsletter]');

    forms.forEach((form) => {
      const email = form.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[type="email"]');
      const err = form.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.nl__err');
      const status = form.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.nl__status');
      const btn = form.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('button[type="submit"]');
      const statusText = status?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.form__status-text') ?? null;
      if (!email || !err || !status || !btn || !statusText) return;

      const idle = btn.textContent ?? 'Subscribe';

      /* [A-269 2026-09-08] One place that changes the outcome region. Four call
         sites set `hidden`, `className` and `textContent` separately, which is
         four chances to leave a stale state class behind a fresh sentence, and
         four copies of the `nl__status` class. That class carries no styling
         today, checked rather than assumed: it is the selector the query above
         uses to find this region, so dropping it on a rebuild would leave the
         next submit unable to find its own status line. */
      const setStatus = (state: 'busy' | 'ok' | 'warn' | 'bad', message: string) => {
        status!.className = `form__status form__status--${state} nl__status`;
        statusText!.textContent = message;
        status!.hidden = false;
      };

      /* aria-busy is what distinguishes a control that is working from one that
         is broken. A disabled button on its own does not say which. */
      const setBusy = (busy: boolean) => {
        btn!.disabled = busy;
        if (busy) btn!.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
        else btn!.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
      };

      /** Error text and aria-invalid move together, so the red border is never
          the only signal that something is wrong. */
      function setError(show: boolean) {
        err!.hidden = !show;
        email!.setAttribute('aria-invalid', show ? 'true' : 'false');
      }

      const value = () => email.value.trim();

      email.addEventListener('blur', () => setError(!EMAIL_RE.test(value())));
      email.addEventListener('input', () => {
        if (EMAIL_RE.test(value())) setError(false);
      });

      form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();

        // A bot filled the field no human can see. Fail silently: an error
        // message here only tells it what to avoid next time.
        const pot = form.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[name="website"]');
        if (pot && pot.value) return;

        // Strip CR/LF: a newline in an address is the classic header-injection
        // vector if the value is ever used to build an email.
        const address = value().replace(/[\r\n]+/g, '');
        if (!EMAIL_RE.test(address)) {
          setError(true);
          email.focus();
          return;
        }
        setError(false);

        /* Cloudflare's own input name, written by their widget. The
           capture-phase gate in Turnstile.astro has already refused this
           submit if it were empty, so this is a read, not a second check. */
        const tsToken =
          form.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[name="cf-turnstile-response"]')?.value ?? null;

        /* [A-269 2026-09-08] The wait is stated rather than left to a dead
           button, which is also what a broken button looks like. */
        setBusy(true);
        btn.textContent = 'Sending...';
        setStatus('busy', 'Adding your address...');

        fetch(form.action, {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', Accept: 'application/json' },
          body: JSON.stringify({
            email: address,
            source: form.dataset.source,
            // Named to match the field /api/contact/submit already verifies, so
            // the endpoint change is a copy rather than a new contract. Zod
            // strips it today; see the warning at the top of this file.
            turnstile_token: tsToken,
          }),
          /* [A-264 2026-09-08] 30s, for the reason measured on the contact
             form: a real submission there returned 200 on the server while the
             reader was shown a failure, because the client aborted at 10s.
             This endpoint is the lighter of the two and has been observed
             answering well inside 10s, so this is headroom rather than a fix
             to an observed failure here. The three forms keep one number so
             the next person changes one thing. */
          signal: AbortSignal.timeout(30000),
        })
          .then(async (r) => {
            // Success ONLY on a 2xx. See the note at the top of this file.
            if (!r.ok) throw new Error(String(r.status));
            /*
             * [A-264 2026-09-08] "You are on the list" IS NOW READ FROM THE
             * RESPONSE, not assumed from the status code.
             *
             * /api/notify answers 2xx when the address was captured EITHER on
             * the Brevo list OR in the ops inbox, and those are two different
             * promises. Only the first one means subscribed. A Brevo key scoped
             * to transactional email without contacts permission, which is a
             * real Brevo configuration, sends the ops mail fine and fails the
             * contacts call, and this line would then have claimed a
             * subscription that did not happen.
             *
             * This is the same rule the top of the file already records for the
             * 404 era, applied one level deeper: the earlier fix stopped us
             * assuming success from a request we never checked, and this one
             * stops us assuming SUBSCRIPTION from a success we did check.
             *
             * `subscribed` is absent on an older API build. Absent is treated as
             * subscribed so a deploy skew cannot downgrade a working signup into
             * a warning, and the field is only ever used to WEAKEN the claim.
             */
            const data = await r.json().catch(() => ({}) as { subscribed?: boolean });
            form.reset();
            /* aria-busy off, disabled on, in both branches. The work has
               finished either way, so announcing it as still working would be
               false, and the field has been reset so a second press would post
               an empty address. */
            setBusy(false);
            btn.disabled = true;
            if (data.subscribed === false) {
              setStatus(
                'warn',
                'Thanks. We have your address and will add you to the list shortly.',
              );
              btn.textContent = 'Received';
              return;
            }
            setStatus('ok', 'Thanks. You are on the list.');
            btn.textContent = 'Subscribed';
          })
          .catch((err) => {
            /*
             * [A-263 2026-09-07] THE ERROR IS LOGGED RATHER THAN DISCARDED.
             *
             * This was `.catch(() => {` and it threw the reason away. On
             * 2026-09-07 the owner reported this form failing on production and
             * the only evidence available anywhere was the sentence below,
             * which is the same sentence for a 502, a 429, a CORS refusal and a
             * dropped connection. Diagnosing it needed the SERVER logs, and the
             * server is a different repository. A reader with the console open
             * could tell us nothing.
             *
             * The cause that day was a Brevo API key returning 401 "Key not
             * found", so /api/notify answered 502. The status was in the reject
             * reason all along, one line above where it was being deleted.
             *
             * The MESSAGE the reader sees is deliberately unchanged: an HTTP
             * status is not something to put in front of somebody trying to
             * subscribe to a newsletter, and the email address is the escape
             * hatch that still works when this endpoint does not.
             */
            console.error(
              JSON.stringify({
                level: 'error',
                service: 'newsletter-form',
                operation: 'subscribe',
                endpoint: form.action,
                source: form.dataset.source ?? null,
                error: err instanceof Error ? `${err.name}: ${err.message}` : String(err),
                timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
              }),
            );
            setStatus(
              'bad',
              'Something went wrong. Please try again, or email hello@crowagent.ai.',
            );
            setBusy(false);
            btn.textContent = idle;
          });
      });
    });
  }

  initNewsletterForms();
</script>

<style>
  /* ── THE FORM STARTS WHERE ITS CARD STARTS, 2026-08-31 ────────────────────
   *
   * This read `margin-inline: auto`, argued as "the form sits under a centred
   * head block, so it centres on the same axis". That was true of the head block
   * it was written under and it is no longer true of the card it is in. Both
   * call sites, /about and /contact, are `.digest` cards, and alignment.css rule
   * 4 puts a box that contains a <form> on the left axis, on the owner
   * instruction of 2026-08-31: a centred box with left aligned lines inside it,
   * labels sharing one left edge.
   *
   * `text-align` COULD NOT HAVE DONE THIS AND THAT IS WHY THE RULE ALONE WAS NOT
   * ENOUGH. Rule 4 moved the digest heading to the card's left edge and left the
   * form exactly where it was, because an auto inline margin is not inline
   * content and text-align cannot reach it. Measured on /about at 1440 after
   * rule 4 landed and before this: heading at x=57, body text starting at x=350,
   * email field at x=580. Three starts in one card, which is a worse reader than
   * the centred version it replaced. The two auto margins had to go with it, and
   * the third, `.digest__p`, is dealt with at each call site.
   *
   * THE 520px CAP STAYS. It is the reason a 1232px card does not present a
   * 1100px-wide email field, and it has nothing to do with where the block
   * sits. */
  .nl {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--stack-1);
    max-width: 520px;
  }

  .nl__row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  /* The input takes the slack; the button keeps its label on one line. */
  .nl__row .field__input {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .nl__submit {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }

  .nl__err {
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* NO `text-align` HERE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE FIX RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
     This read `center` under the same superseded assumption as the auto margin
     above, and the first correction replaced it with `left`. That was the wrong
     shape twice over: check-design-system rule 1 fires on a local
     `text-align: left` inside a `.surface`, correctly, because a per-component
     opt-out with no argument behind it is exactly what styles/alignment.css
     exists to end. There is nothing to opt out of any more. The `.digest` card
     around this line holds a <form>, so alignment.css rule 4 puts the whole card
     on the left axis and this paragraph INHERITS it, along with the heading, the
     body and the field. One declaration in one place, and the legal line lands
     on the same left edge as everything above it by inheritance. */
  .nl__legal {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .nl__legal a {
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  /* Below this the two controls stack, because a 52px input and a pill button
     side by side leave the input too narrow to read an address in. */
  @media (max-width: 519px) {
    .nl__row {
      flex-direction: column;
    }
  }

</style>
